#Evidence

Everything the teaser claims about notlob, it shows by running the real
thing. This module gathers that evidence from pn-chomper: the results of
actually running its claims, measurements of how much an agent would
have to read with and without the name-graph, and the states of a real
Petri net produced by pn-chomper's own firing rule. Nothing here is
typed in by hand; if pn-chomper changes, the video changes with it.

The star of the teaser is one module of that project.

    STAR_PROJECT = Path("../ref-projects/pn-chomper")
    STAR_MODULE = STAR_PROJECT / "petri" / "marking.lob"
    WORK = Path("../work")

##Claim Results

notlob reports each claim it runs as a line beginning with its status
and the source position of the assertion -- one line per assertion for
examples and tests, one per property at its sigil. The status by source
line is exactly what is needed to put a tick beside the line on screen.

    RESULT_LINE = re.compile(r"^(PASS|FAIL|ERROR)\s+\S+?:(\d+)\s")

    def parse_results(output: str) -> dict:
        results = {}
        for line in output.splitlines():
            match = RESULT_LINE.match(line)
            if match:
                results[int(match.group(2))] = match.group(1)
        return results

~example
    parse_results("PASS   marking.lob:136  x\nnoise\nFAIL   marking.lob:9  y") == {136: "PASS", 9: "FAIL"}

The claims are run in the project itself, with its own toolchain, the
way its author runs them.

    @cache
    def claim_results(project: Path, module: str) -> dict:
        # check=False: a failing claim is a result to show, not an error here.
        result = subprocess.run(["notlob", "test", module], cwd=project,
                                capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        return parse_results(result.stdout)

##Context

The question an agent might be asked about pn-chomper: how does firing
a transition work? Without the name-graph, a common way in is to search
the sources for the word and read each file that matches. With it, the
agent asks notlob for the one section that answers the question. Both
are measured in characters of text the agent takes in. The search side
assumes every matching file is read whole; an agent may read less, so
this is the cost of the plain approach rather than its floor.

    QUESTION = "How does firing a transition work?"
    SEARCH_WORD = "fire"
    ANSWER_ADDRESS = "petri/marking#Enabling and Firing"

A search reports each matching line with its file and line number.

    def search_hits(sources: dict, word: str) -> str:
        pattern = re.compile(rf"\b{re.escape(word)}\b")
        return "".join(
            f"{name}:{number}:{line}\n"
            for name, text in sorted(sources.items())
            for number, line in enumerate(text.split("\n"), 1)
            if pattern.search(line)
        )

~example
    search_hits({"a.lob": "x\nfire it\nfired", "b.lob": "no"}, "fire") == "a.lob:2:fire it\n"

The plain approach costs the search output plus every file it matched.

    def _sources(project: Path) -> dict:
        return {str(p.relative_to(project)): p.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
                for p in sorted(project.glob("**/*.lob"))}

    def search_files(project: Path, word: str) -> list:
        hits = search_hits(_sources(project), word)
        return sorted({line.split(":", 1)[0] for line in hits.splitlines()})

    def search_context(project: Path, word: str) -> int:
        sources = _sources(project)
        hits = search_hits(sources, word)
        return len(hits) + sum(len(sources[name]) for name in search_files(project, word))

Between the two sits the cost of one module read whole -- the essay,
the code and the claims together, which is what a literate source file
puts in an agent's context window at once.

    def file_context(path: Path) -> int:
        return len(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))

The notlob approach costs what the query returns.

    def query_context(project: Path, address: str) -> int:
        result = subprocess.run(["notlob", "query", "content", address], cwd=project,
                                capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)
        return len(result.stdout)

##The Split

The second example in Petri Marking checks a split: one token in place
p1 fires through transition t1, and afterwards p2 and p3 hold one each.
To draw it, the net and both markings are taken from pn-chomper's own
code. The module is built by notlob into a working directory of ours,
a few lines are added that print the net and the markings as JSON, and
the result is run with pn-chomper's own TypeScript runner.

    DUMP = """
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      places: [..._testNet.places.values()],
      transitions: [..._testNet.transitions.values()],
      arcs: _testNet.arcs,
      before: Object.fromEntries(_m0),
      after: Object.fromEntries(_m1),
    }));
    """

    @cache
    def split_states(project: Path, module: str) -> dict:
        out = (WORK / project.name).resolve()
        # check=False: pn-chomper's on-build hook expects a whole-game build and
        # warns on a single module; the module itself is still built.
        subprocess.run(["notlob", "build", "--skip-tests", "--output", str(out), module],
                       cwd=project, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        built = out / (module.removesuffix(".lob").replace("/", "_") + ".ts")
        script = out / "split_dump.ts"
        script.write_text(built.read_text(encoding="utf-8") + DUMP, encoding="utf-8")
        runner = project.resolve() / "node_modules" / "tsx" / "dist" / "cli.mjs"
        result = subprocess.run(["node", str(runner), str(script)],
                                capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)
        return json.loads(result.stdout)

Run for real, pn-chomper's firing rule gives exactly the split its
example states.

~example
    split_states(STAR_PROJECT, "petri/marking.lob")["before"] == {"p1": 1}
    split_states(STAR_PROJECT, "petri/marking.lob")["after"] == {"p1": 0, "p2": 1, "p3": 1}

---

#Tests

##the claims really pass

Every claim in the star module runs, and passes.

    len(claim_results(STAR_PROJECT, "petri/marking.lob")) > 0
    set(claim_results(STAR_PROJECT, "petri/marking.lob").values()) == {"PASS"}

##the context comparison favours the query

    query_context(STAR_PROJECT, ANSWER_ADDRESS) * 5 < search_context(STAR_PROJECT, SEARCH_WORD)

##one module costs less than the search, more than the query

    query_context(STAR_PROJECT, ANSWER_ADDRESS) < file_context(STAR_MODULE) < search_context(STAR_PROJECT, SEARCH_WORD)

##the split net is the whole test net

    len(split_states(STAR_PROJECT, "petri/marking.lob")["places"]) == 3
    len(split_states(STAR_PROJECT, "petri/marking.lob")["arcs"]) == 3

#References
    import json
    import re
    import subprocess
    from functools import cache
    from pathlib import Path
